1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,030
This is Tessera. The page reads the
Solana chain directly in my browser.

2
00:00:06,225 --> 00:00:10,155
Squares are merchant agents,
and circles are buyers.

3
00:00:10,205 --> 00:00:14,247
A coin on a line is money held in escrow.

4
00:00:14,297 --> 00:00:18,167
Every wallet has a public credit file.

5
00:00:28,977 --> 00:00:33,851
Here is the top merchant, and
what its score is made of.

6
00:00:33,901 --> 00:00:40,291
Every review shows the weight it carried.
Each one needed a real settled order.

7
00:00:40,808 --> 00:00:46,478
Now the Market. I get test funds
for this browser’s test wallet.

8
00:00:52,208 --> 00:00:56,078
I pick a merchant and press Buy.

9
00:01:03,438 --> 00:01:06,809
The page checks the order on-chain before it pays,
and pays into the escrow, not to the merchant.

10
00:01:06,859 --> 00:01:11,326
The merchant delivers, and the page checks the
delivery against the hash the merchant committed.

11
00:01:11,376 --> 00:01:19,206
This is a fresh wallet, so the money is held.
I confirm receipt, and the merchant is paid.

12
00:01:22,746 --> 00:01:30,216
Then I leave a review. It is stored on-chain,
with the weight of the money behind it.

13
00:01:45,475 --> 00:01:51,865
The score is integer arithmetic over public
accounts, the same code the program runs.

14
00:01:56,053 --> 00:02:00,643
A wallet that only sells to itself stays low.

15
00:02:03,771 --> 00:02:09,081
A busy wallet with real customers
climbs, and its hold shrinks.

16
00:02:11,572 --> 00:02:16,882
An agent does the same from
a terminal, with no website.

17
00:02:19,775 --> 00:02:25,445
One call ranks merchants by
reviews that each cost a real sale.

18
00:02:29,776 --> 00:02:33,237
Recorded on a local Solana validator
running the real Tessera program.

19
00:02:33,287 --> 00:02:36,739
Devnet only, test money, one
arbiter key, no third-party audit.
